<ui version="4.0">
 <class>GaussianSplatSettingsWidget</class>
 <widget class="QWidget" name="GaussianSplatSettingsWidget">
  <property name="geometry">
   <rect>
    <x>0</x>
    <y>0</y>
    <width>340</width>
    <height>440</height>
   </rect>
  </property>
  <property name="windowTitle">
   <string>Form</string>
  </property>
  <layout class="QFormLayout" name="formLayout">
   <item row="0" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="preProcessingSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Pre-processing</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Rebuilds the cloud or its LoD tree. Seconds of work on a large capture, and the result is what every frame afterwards draws.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="1" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="sceneSplatsLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Scene splats</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Pick a Gaussian splat object in the world to select it (its transform gizmo appears). Refreshed while the panel is open. Convenience tool when a scene has many splats and clicking the right one in the viewport is awkward.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="1" column="1">
    <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="sceneSplatsHorizontalLayout">
     <item>
      <widget class="QComboBox" name="sceneSplatsComboBox">
       <property name="sizePolicy">
        <sizepolicy hsizetype="Expanding" vsizetype="Fixed">
         <horstretch>0</horstretch>
         <verstretch>0</verstretch>
        </sizepolicy>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Pick a Gaussian splat object in the world to select it (its transform gizmo appears). Refreshed while the panel is open.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QCheckBox" name="hideSplatCheckBox">
       <property name="text">
        <string>Hide</string>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Debug tool: leave the object currently picked above out of the render pipeline entirely, without changing its saved data. Per-object, always off at the start of a session - never saved.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item row="2" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lodBaseLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>LoD base</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Voxel-grid growth factor between tree levels, used when a splat object's LoD tree is built. A tree already built keeps the lod_base it was built with even after this changes - press Rebuild to re-build every loaded splat object's tree at the current value instead of reloading/re-adding them.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="2" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="lodBaseWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="lodBaseHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="lodBaseDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Voxel-grid growth factor between tree levels, used when a splat object's LoD tree is built. A tree already built keeps the lod_base it was built with even after this changes - press Rebuild to re-build every loaded splat object's tree at the current value instead of reloading/re-adding them.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>1.01000000000000001</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>4.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QPushButton" name="rebuildLodsPushButton">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Rebuild</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION073: re-builds every registered splat object's LoD tree in place at the LoD base above, without reloading/re-adding the object or restarting the client. Synchronous - seconds of work on a multi-million-splat scene, same cost class as "Merge coplanar" below. Does not touch or undo a coplanar merge; only the tree.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="3" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="mergeLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Merge:</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How alike two splats have to be before "Merge coplanar" will treat them as the same surface seen twice. Colour is the allowed difference per channel (0-1); angle is the allowed difference in facing, in degrees. Loosening either shows up as colour bleeding and as surfaces thickening - a disc tilted by the angle tolerance contributes that much of its own radius across the surface it is merged onto, which is what makes the merged cloud less flat. Measured acceptable setting on the reference capture: 0.2 and 20.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="3" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="mergeParamsWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="mergeParamsHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeColourTolDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Colour: Allowed difference in colour per channel, on the 0-1 scale. Loosening it merges splats that do not look alike, which shows up as colour bleeding across a surface. Measured acceptable setting on the reference capture: 0.2.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>col </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.001000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.150000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeAngleTolDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Angle: Allowed difference in facing, in degrees. This is the knob that decides how flat the merged cloud stays: a disc tilted by this much contributes about 0.7 of that tilt as thickness across the surface it is merged onto, and no amount of flattening removes it, because it is the member's own shape rather than its position. Measured acceptable setting: 20.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>ang </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> deg</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>90.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>20.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeAcrossDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Across: Reach along the surface, in centimetres of world space. This is the whole prize and the whole cost. Measured on the reference capture: 1 cm saves about 5% of fill, 2 cm 13%, 4 cm 34% - and the picture visibly softens from 2 cm up, because a group this wide is replaced by one splat that has to cover all of it. Accepted setting: 1.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>acr </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> cm</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>100.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.500000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeThroughDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Through: Reach at right angles to the surface, in centimetres of world space - how deep a stack the merge will collapse. Nearly free in quality, and nearly free in saving too: measured, it saturates by about 10 cm and buys nothing past it (5 to 40 cm moved fill from 3.3% to 5.2%, and the largest group found did not grow at all), because a captured surface is a thin shell rather than a deep stack. Large values also get very slow. Accepted setting: 10.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>thr </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> cm</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>100.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.500000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>8.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="4" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="mergeTailWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="mergeTailHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QPushButton" name="mergeCoplanarPushButton">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Merge coplanar</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Collapses groups of near-duplicate splats - same place, same facing, same colour, within the reach and tolerances above - into one splat each, and rebuilds the LoD trees. A group is only collapsed if the replacement paints less than the members did, so a capture without real stacking is mostly left alone rather than being made worse. Applies to every splat object in the world, takes seconds on a large capture, and does not touch the files: 'Restore unmerged' puts it back. Take a frustum report before and after from the same spot - the "blended" line and the layers-to-saturation figure are the answer, splat count is not.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QPushButton" name="restoreUnmergedPushButton">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Restore unmerged</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Puts every merged cloud back to the splats it was loaded with, so the merged and unmerged pictures can be compared from a fixed camera.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="mergeFlattenCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Flatten</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Collapse each merged group onto the surface it lies on, instead of fitting a splat around where its members actually sat. Without this, the depth of a stack ends up as the thickness of its replacement - a 20 cm deep stack of flat discs becomes a 20 cm thick lens, which costs nothing seen face-on but stops looking like a surface, and is what makes a generous 'through' go soft. With it, the replacement stays as thin as its members and only their sideways spread widens it. Unchecked reproduces the earlier behaviour, for comparison.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QLabel" name="mergeCoplanarResultLabel">
        <property name="text">
         <string></string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="5" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QFrame" name="cpuSideSeparatorLine">
     <property name="frameShape">
      <enum>QFrame::HLine</enum>
     </property>
     <property name="frameShadow">
      <enum>QFrame::Sunken</enum>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="6" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="cpuSideSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>CPU side</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>SESSION075: settings for the stage while data is still on the CPU, deciding what to send to the GPU this frame - LoD selection, traversal, culling, dilation, the saturation pre-filter. Nothing here touches a shader.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="7" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="pixelScaleLimitLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Pixel scale limit</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Traversal stops refining a node once its on-screen footprint is below this many pixels. Lower = more detail, more splats drawn. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="7" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="pixelScaleLimitDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Traversal stops refining a node once its on-screen footprint is below this many pixels. Lower = more detail, more splats drawn. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>50.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <double>2.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="8" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lodSettings1Label">
     <property name="text">
      <string>LoD settings1</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Merged LoD node colour derivation and geometric anti-ripple widening - see each control's own tooltip.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="8" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="lodSettings1Widget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="lodSettings1HorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="energyMergeColourCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>energy merge</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION071: derive a merged LoD node's colour + opacity from conservation of on-screen premultiplied energy (weight = opacity * silhouette AREA; opacity = 1 - (1 - mean_alpha)^layers) instead of the session063 volume-ratio amplitude. Fixes two artefacts that grow with the pixel scale limit: bright dense regions merging to flat clipped white (the old A&gt;1 branch scaled colour into the clamp), and widely-spread groups going transparent and dark (the old amplitude divided by a parent VOLUME inflated by the merge's spread-of-centres term). Re-derives colours on the loaded scene - no reload, no re-traversal. Applies live.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="mergeSpreadWidenDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>widen </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION071: how much wider than plain moment-matching a MERGED LoD node's Gaussian is drawn, applied along the spread of its children only - leaves keep their authored size and a flat surface keeps its thickness, so widening does not turn a wall into fog. At 1 (plain moment-matching) the merge produces sigma = L/sqrt(12) = 0.29*L for children filling a cell of width L, while smooth coverage needs sigma/d &gt;~ 0.5; the resulting ~30% ripple in the sum of neighbouring Gaussians shows as a blurred lattice on flat surfaces and reads as darkening, because compositing is concave - peaks lose their excess to saturation while troughs let the dark background through. Isolated on the ablation ladder: stage 7 (flat alpha) keeps the brightness, stage 8 (Gaussian falloff) loses it. Analytic value sqrt(3) = 1.73 is the point where sigma just meets the smooth-coverage threshold; owner default is set higher for extra margin. Re-bakes on change (no traversal).</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>3.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="9" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="maxSplatsBudgetLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Max splats budget</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-cloud cap on how many LoD tree nodes one traversal may select. If a cloud's diagnostics show "!WARNING! budget cap", this is limiting detail on that cloud even though pixel_scale_limit hasn't converged. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="9" column="1">
    <widget class="QSpinBox" name="maxSplatsBudgetSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-cloud cap on how many LoD tree nodes one traversal may select. If a cloud's diagnostics show "!WARNING! budget cap", this is limiting detail on that cloud even though pixel_scale_limit hasn't converged. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <number>1000</number>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <number>999999999</number>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <number>100000</number>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="10" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="resortMoveThresholdLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Resort move threshold (m)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How far the camera has to move (scaled by distance to the cloud) before a cloud's LoD frontier is re-traversed. Lower = frontier updates more often, at more CPU cost. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="10" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="resortMoveThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How far the camera has to move (scaled by distance to the cloud) before a cloud's LoD frontier is re-traversed. Lower = frontier updates more often, at more CPU cost. Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.01000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>100.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.01000000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="11" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lodCapsLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>LoD caps</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Two ceilings on how far LoD traversal may unfold, both independent of pixel scale limit, both 0 = disabled. Density: a node estimated to be this dense with overdraw (the same layer-count scale the debug ramp uses) is kept as its own merged approximation rather than refined further - find a problem area's layer count with the debug view, then set this a bit below it. Depth: a hard global ceiling on levels from the tree root, everywhere in the world. Both apply live, on the next camera movement.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="11" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="lodCapsWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="lodCapsHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="maxLayerDensityDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>density </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Caps LoD traversal: a node estimated to be this dense with overdraw (see the "Show overdraw" ramp above - the same layer-count scale) is kept as its own merged approximation rather than refined further, regardless of pixel scale limit. Use the overdraw view to find a problem area's layer count, then set this a bit below it. 0 = disabled (no effect on the visualisation above - only on real rendering). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="maxTreeDepthSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>depth </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Hard global ceiling on how many levels traversal may unfold from the tree root, everywhere in the world - independent of pixel scale limit / max layer density / max splats budget. A node at this depth is always kept as its own merged approximation, never refined further. 0 = disabled (no ceiling). Applies live, on the next camera movement.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>1000</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="13" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="splitPipelineLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Split pipeline</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>SESSION063 split-filter pipeline (SESSION075: separated from "cull" below, previously the same checkbox). On (default): traversal runs cull-off and builds an orientation-independent frontier U(P) once per camera move; a cheap per-frame filter then derives the actual draw list from it, so turning in place costs nothing extra. Off: reverts to the pre-SESSION063 single-pass pipeline - no U(P), no per-frame filter, no coarse floor, no saturation pre-filter; each significant camera move re-walks and re-draws the selection directly, with "cull" below then controlling the traversal's own in-loop frustum test instead of the filter's.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="13" column="1">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="splitPipelineCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string></string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>See this row's own tooltip.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="checked">
      <bool>true</bool>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="14" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="saturationFilterLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Saturation filter</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>SESSION074 pre-GPU occlusion cull. Estimates, from the coarse LoD floor, which directions from the camera are already fully covered by opaque geometry, and drops fine nodes that lie behind that cover before they ever cost a GPU vertex - not just their blend cost, unlike the saturation gate (GPU side, below). SESSION075: independent of "coarse" (Frustum settings2, below) - traversal captures the coarse-floor data this stage needs whenever this is on, whether or not "coarse" is also on to actually draw that layer as an edge-filling patch. Off by default.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="14" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="saturationFilterWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="saturationFilterHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="saturationFilterCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string></string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>See this row's own tooltip.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>false</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="satPrefilterThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>thr </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION079. This stage's OWN saturation threshold - independent of the "Saturation gate" row's threshold below, which this used to reuse. Coverage a direction must reach, from the coarse floor's accumulated alpha, before this CPU pre-GPU cull considers it occluded and starts dropping fine geometry behind it. Split out into its own control because the two mechanisms are genuinely independent (this one is a once-per-traversal CPU decision from the coarse floor; the gate below is a per-frame GPU test) and the owner expects the gate may eventually be dropped, at which point this one needs to keep standing on its own. Default 0.98.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>4</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>0.999900000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.010000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.980000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="coarsePixelScaleDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>px </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION063 K4: pixel scale threshold for the coarse floor cut. The first node on each branch whose projected size falls to this many pixels is taken as the coarse patch (bigger = coarser, fewer, cheaper). SESSION076: also sets the saturation grid's resolution (moved here from Frustum settings2 since both consumers share this one value). Applies live.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>2.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>500.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>25.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="satGridSubdivDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>sub </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION076 CALIBRATION. Divides the saturation grid's tile angular size, so grid resolution scales with it. Silhouette accuracy is bounded by tile size: at 1 a tile spans the whole "px" value (30 px at the default), which cannot separate a 30-50 px feature - a chair back protruding above a table - from the geometry beside it in the same tile, so the nearer one saturates the tile and takes the other with it. Higher = finer silhouettes, more tiles, more grid build cost (off the critical path since the saturation pass was split out of the traversal). SESSION078: default is 0.3, the owner's own aggressive pick after visually verifying the octahedral tile-angle fix (GaussianSplatSaturationGrid.cpp) on this session's problem scene. Coarser than this does show small artifacts on close inspection - there's headroom left for further tuning - but the owner chose to stop here rather than keep dotuning. Pair with "at" 0.99, not 0.96 - the lower threshold visibly strengthens this setting's artifacts.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.010000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>8.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.500000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.300000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="satRegionRadiusDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>R </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION078 REGION PRUNING. Radius, in world units, of the ball of camera positions the saturation barrier is made valid for. 0 = the old behaviour: the barrier describes the single point it was built from, so it goes stale the instant the camera moves, showing as shadow-shaped holes. Owner-measured working range: 0.01-0.03, with no visible artifacts in motion above ~0.03 - much smaller than a naive "full latency window x typical speed" estimate (which would suggest ~0.6), because what actually needs covering is the INCREMENT between one traversal's anchor and the next re-kick's, not the whole gap from a standing start - re-kicks fire far more often than once per latency window during continuous motion. Strictly weakens the cull: both sides widen their conservatism, so it can only ever drop fewer nodes than 0 does - watch dropped= in [gsr-sat] for what it costs. The penalty is R/d in angle, so it is nearly free far away and expensive up close, which is the useful shape here (this stage drops distant geometry; the occluders doing the work are near).</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.010000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="satDiagCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>diag</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION076/078 DIAGNOSTIC. Prints an extra [gsr-sat-diag] console line breaking down what the coarse capture is actually buying this stage: cA/cB splits the captured nodes into the genuinely low-detail cut (captured for being at or below "px") and the near-field nodes captured only because their branch hit a leaf while still coarser than that - the latter are duplicates of nodes already in the fine frontier, present only because the drawn coarse patch needs a complete cover, which this grid does not. writers is how many captured nodes the grid actually accepted (a node contributes only if its footprint fully covers at least one tile), against pred=, the same count derived analytically - they must agree. Needs the filter log on, and Saturation filter on. Pure console counting - unrelated to the "Saturation mask"/"Saturation ramp" visual overlay in the Diagnostics section's debug mode dropdown, which has its own gate (Show debug + that dropdown). Own checkbox rather than more fields on [gsr-sat] because the counting perturbs the very loops being timed. Not saved between sessions.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>false</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="15" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="frustumSettings1Label">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Frustum settings1</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Frustum cull toggle and the split-filter's fine-set dilation timing - see each control's own tooltip.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="15" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="frustumSettings1Widget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="frustumSettings1HorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="cullCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>cull</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION075: cull by the camera frustum. Which mechanism actually does the work depends on Split pipeline (CPU side, above): with it on, this drives the per-orientation filter's frustum test (SESSION074) over the cached U(P) - cheap, reruns every rotating frame. With it off, this drives the traversal's own in-loop cull instead (SESSION055) - nodes outside the frustum are neither drawn nor expanded, pruning whole subtrees, at the cost of a fresh traversal on every ~5deg turn. On by default.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="filterDilationLatencyDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>lat </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>latency: SESSION063 K3: split-filter dilation window (seconds). The filter keeps nodes this much "ahead" of the camera's motion so the frustum edge doesn't trail during the filter's latency. Bigger = the edge holds under fast rotation/translation but more off-screen nodes are kept. The old cull path used 0.3. SESSION072: default raised 0.06->0.17 - the ~13ms figure elsewhere is just the filter task's own compute; the actual kick-to-drain round trip measured during a fast flick (millions of survivors to issue/draw) was 136-166ms. Applies live.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>2.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.020000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.170000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="filterMinRotRateDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>rot </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Units: deg/s. SESSION063 K3: baseline angular rate the split-filter dilation assumes even from a standstill, so the static-&gt;moving transition is covered before the motion tracker catches up. Applies live.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>720.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>10.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="filterMaxRotRateDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>cap </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>rotation cap: Units: deg/s. REQUIRED: must be greater than "rot" (the min rate floor) - if not, the directional (anisotropic) padding can never exceed the floor's own isotropic padding, so max(floor, directional) always picks the floor and the directional term becomes dead weight - no crash, just quietly back to old-style uniform padding on every plane.

SESSION071/072: cap on the angular rate the split-filter dilation uses for its directional (anisotropic) term - see "rot"'s own tooltip for the floor it's capped against. Lowering this bounds dilation cost during a violent flick at the price of edge holes. Default (100000) is effectively uncapped. Applies live.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>15.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>100000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>20.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>40.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="filterMinTransRateDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>trans </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Units: m/s. SESSION063 K3: baseline translation rate the split-filter dilation assumes even from a standstill, covering the static-&gt;moving transition for camera movement. Applies live.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>100.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.500000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>10.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="16" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="frustumSettings2Label">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Frustum settings2</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>The split-filter's coarse floor cut - see each control's own tooltip.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="16" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="frustumSettings2Widget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="frustumSettings2HorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="coarseFloorCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>coarse</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION063 K4: the traversal also captures a low-detail cut (first node per branch at the coarse pixel scale), globally depth-sorted with the fine set. Confined to the dilation band beyond the frustum edge, its big cheap splats plug motion-revealed gaps/edges - dilated wider than the fine set (coarse dil latency below), which lets the fine set run at a tight, cheap dilation without holes. On by default. SESSION075: controls only whether this layer is drawn - Saturation filter (above) captures the same underlying data on its own whenever it's on, whether or not this is. SESSION076: the pixel scale threshold ("px") now lives on the Saturation filter row above, since it also sets that stage's grid resolution.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="coarseDilationLatencyDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>dil </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> s</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>REQUIRED: must be greater than "latency" (Frustum settings1) - if not, the coarse band collapses to zero or negative width and coarse stops covering anything fine doesn't already reach, defeating its whole purpose as a cheap wide safety net.

SESSION063 K4: dilation window (seconds) for the coarse floor - how far ahead of the camera's motion the coarse patches are kept. Applies live.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.900000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="coarseLayerDebugCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>debug</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION063 K4 debug: draw ONLY the coarse floor, hiding the fine set, so its screen coverage can be checked in isolation (should fill the frame with no edge gaps under motion). Visual debug only - turn off for normal rendering.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>false</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="17" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QFrame" name="gpuSideSeparatorLine">
     <property name="frameShape">
      <enum>QFrame::HLine</enum>
     </property>
     <property name="frameShadow">
      <enum>QFrame::Sunken</enum>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="18" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="gpuSideSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>GPU side</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>SESSION075: settings for the stage once the CPU has finished deciding what to draw and the fixed list is handed to the GPU - shading, blending, the saturation gate, post-processing. Nothing here changes which splats are selected.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="19" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="dofDepthModeLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>DoF mode</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How Gaussian splats contribute depth to Depth of Field (and fog): the splat rasteriser normally does not write depth at all, so DoF blurs splats regardless of focus distance and only picks them up when focus distance happens to match whatever was behind them. This setting picks between three ways to fill that gap. Only engages when DoF blur is on - zero cost when it is off, regardless of the mode.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="19" column="1">
    <widget class="QComboBox" name="dofDepthModeComboBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>off: upstream behaviour, splats do not write depth at all - DoF blurs them regardless of focus distance. Kept for A/B comparison.

prepass: a second, colour-masked depth-only pass over every visible splat after the composite, using a strict alpha threshold so only meaningfully opaque fragments write depth. Cheap - one extra geometry pass per DoF frame - but the answer is discrete: silhouettes can step visibly where the frontmost splat changes.

weighted: accumulate depth * alpha * transmittance into a second colour attachment during the splat pass, resolve pass divides through by coverage and writes gl_FragDepth. Smooth by construction, no silhouette steps, at the cost of a second attachment blended alongside the accumulation buffer. Not physically the depth of a surface: where a lacy foreground shows a background through it, the mean depth lands between them, where nothing actually is.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="currentIndex">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>off</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>prepass</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>weighted</string>
      </property>
     </item>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="12" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="alphaAdjustLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Alpha adjustments</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Rescales every splat's own opacity before anything else looks at it: alpha' = gain * alpha^gamma, clamped to 1. Applied at the earliest point that can still be live - in the vertex shader, before the quad is sized - so it moves the drawn area as well as the pixel's colour, and everything downstream (the saturation gate, the overdraw views, the frustum report) sees the adjusted value. SESSION078: also reaches the CPU "Saturation filter" prefilter below - with "ignore" off, raising alpha in a translucent region genuinely raises how much of it that prefilter can prune, since it now sees the same opacity the renderer draws. Both at 1 is the cloud as captured. Note it does not reach the LoD tree, which was built from the stored alpha; rebuild the tree to fold a setting in permanently. Moved here from the GPU section (SESSION078) - conceptually a CPU-visible knob now, not purely a draw-time one.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="12" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="alphaAdjustWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="alphaAdjustHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="alphaGainDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Multiplies every splat's opacity, after the gamma below. Above 1 makes the cloud denser - fewer layers are needed to cover a pixel, so the saturation gate finishes pixels sooner, but quads also grow, since a splat is drawn out to where its alpha decays to the alpha cutoff. Below 1 thins it out. 1 = as captured.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>gain </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.010000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>20.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="alphaGammaDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Exponent applied to opacity before the gain: alpha^gamma. Below 1 lifts the faint splats towards the opaque ones (the low-opacity bulk the fill sits in); above 1 pushes them down and leaves only what was already solid. This is the knob that changes the shape of the opacity distribution rather than just its scale - see the "Fill by opacity" histogram in the frustum report. 1 = as captured.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>gamma </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="alphaAdjustIgnoreCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>ignore</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Sends gain and gamma as 1 and 1 without touching what the boxes say, so a setting can be switched in and out against the same camera. Not remembered between sessions: it is a comparison, not a preference.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="21" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="alphaCutoffLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Alpha cutoff</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-splat quad radius is cut to exactly where its alpha decays to this value, instead of a fixed 3-sigma bound - 1/255 = 0.0039 matches the fragment shader's own discard and is lossless; the shipped default (0.0201) trims low-opacity splats' quads for less overdraw, at the cost of their faintest edge. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="21" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="alphaCutoffDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Per-splat quad radius is cut to exactly where its alpha decays to this value, instead of a fixed 3-sigma bound - 1/255 = 0.0039 matches the fragment shader's own discard and is lossless; the shipped default (0.0201) trims low-opacity splats' quads for less overdraw, at the cost of their faintest edge. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>4</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.000100000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>0.500000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.001000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <double>0.020100000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="22" column="0">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="ewaProjectionFixCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Fix EWA projection</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>The splat projection is an affine approximation of the perspective divide, taken about each splat&apos;s centre, and it is only valid while the splat is small compared with its distance. Off (unchecked) is the projection exactly as it was before any of this, for A/B comparison. On applies three corrections: splats whose bounding sphere is entirely outside the frustum are dropped before the covariance is built; the screen radius is bounded by what the splat&apos;s own size and distance geometrically allow, instead of only by twice the viewport; and a splat the camera is getting inside of is faded out, since part of it is then behind the camera and its projection does not exist. The last of those is what the fade width beside this controls.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="22" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="ewaProjectionFixWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="ewaProjectionFixHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="nearFadeWidthDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>near fade </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Fades out a splat as the camera gets inside its own extent, i.e. as the distance to its centre falls below its 3-sigma radius. Part of such a splat is behind the camera, where the perspective divide throws it above the horizon instead of below, and it is drawn as a streak climbing through the frame - the projection of a splat straddling the camera plane does not exist, so no radius bound can fix it. Big flat floor and wall splats hit this at a metre or two. This is how wide the fade is as a fraction of that ratio: 0 = a hard cull the moment it happens (splats pop as you walk), 0.3 (default) = fade over the last 30% of the approach. Costs nothing and saves area, since the quad shrinks with the opacity.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.300000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="23" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="numDrawSlicesLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Draw slices</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How many consecutive sub-ranges each cloud's depth-sorted splats are drawn in, nearest first. 1 = one draw per cloud. On its own this must not change the image at all: the ranges are drawn back to back in the same order, so any value should look identical. It exists so a front-to-back saturation test has somewhere to run between draws. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="23" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="drawSlicesWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="drawSlicesHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="numDrawSlicesSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>How many consecutive sub-ranges each cloud's depth-sorted splats are drawn in, nearest first. 1 = one draw per cloud. On its own this must not change the image at all: the ranges are drawn back to back in the same order, so any value should look identical. It exists so a front-to-back saturation test has somewhere to run between draws. Applies live, immediately.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>1024</number>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <number>6</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="visibleSlicingCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>by visible</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Places the slice boundaries so each slice holds an equal share of the splats actually in frustum, instead of an equal share of the draw order. The draw order is sorted by distance and covers the whole cloud including what is behind you, so the nearest splats by distance are a sphere around the camera - standing above a forest looking into the distance, the first slices are almost all ground below and behind, and the saturation census after each of them photographs a mask that is still completely black. Nothing is culled and the whole order is still drawn: only where it is cut moves, so this cannot change the picture, only where the censuses happen. Costs a few thousand point-in-frustum tests per cloud per frame, whatever the cloud's size. Off = the plain index split, for A/B against the same camera.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="drawSliceLimitSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Diagnostic: stops after this many slices, so the frame is deliberately incomplete - with 6 slices and a limit of 3, only the nearest half of every cloud is drawn. Raise it one step at a time and watch the splat pass time: the step that costs the most is the depth range the fill is actually in. 0 = no limit, the whole cloud, which is the only setting the picture is correct at.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>limit </string>
        </property>
        <property name="specialValueText">
         <string>limit off</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>1024</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QComboBox" name="coverageReduceModeComboBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>How the coverage pyramid the shrink reads is reduced from one level to the next. It sits in this row because it is a property of the census machinery, not of the shrink formula that consumes it - both shrink modes read whichever answer this produces.&#10;&#10;mean (the original): a coarse texel is the average coverage under it. In the middle of a solid surface that is fine. At the edge of one it is not: a splat straddling the boundary between finished and unfinished screen gets an answer describing neither half, and over the unfinished half it is too high, so the splat truncates itself as if those pixels were done. On a low-opacity splat the truncation radius reaches zero at a modest threshold and the splat vanishes outright rather than being trimmed - which is what an unfinished region ringed by finished ones (a cushion against a wall) shows as holes full of flickering splat-sized rectangles.&#10;&#10;min: a coarse texel is the least covered pixel under it, so the answer becomes a lower bound and the straddling splat above draws in full. The error changes direction - the shrink can then only fail to save work, never take light that was needed. This is the rule the binary gate's own pyramid has always used.&#10;&#10;Left on mean by default so earlier sessions' numbers reproduce. Watch the difference with the "Coverage Ln" debug views.</string>
        </property>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>cov mean</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>cov min</string>
         </property>
        </item>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="24" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="sliceGrowthLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Slice growth</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How much bigger each draw slice is than the one before it. 1 = all slices the same size; below 1 = a coarse first slice and a finely sliced tail. Decides where the saturation test gets to look. Nothing is saturated over roughly the first fifth of a depth-sorted cloud (it takes about 63 layers at the measured mean alpha), so a boundary inside that region costs a full-screen pass and skips nothing, while every check past it catches a new batch of finished pixels. Changes nothing on its own, like the slice count itself. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="24" column="1">
    <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="sliceGrowthDoubleSpinBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>How much bigger each draw slice is than the one before it. 1 = all slices the same size; below 1 = a coarse first slice and a finely sliced tail. Decides where the saturation test gets to look. Nothing is saturated over roughly the first fifth of a depth-sorted cloud (it takes about 63 layers at the measured mean alpha), so a boundary inside that region costs a full-screen pass and skips nothing, while every check past it catches a new batch of finished pixels. Changes nothing on its own, like the slice count itself. Applies live, immediately.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="decimals">
      <number>2</number>
     </property>
     <property name="minimum">
      <double>0.250000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="maximum">
      <double>16.000000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="singleStep">
      <double>0.050000000000000</double>
     </property>
     <property name="value">
      <double>1.300000000000000</double>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="25" column="0">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="saturationGateCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Saturation gate</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Skips splats at pixels the front-to-back composite has already finished with: between draw slices, pixels whose accumulated coverage has reached the threshold are marked, and later slices are rejected there before being shaded. Needs more than one draw slice to do anything. Off in the overdraw views, where the accumulated alpha counts layers rather than coverage.</string>
     </property>
     <property name="checked">
      <bool>true</bool>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="25" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="saturationWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="saturationHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="saturationThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>at </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Accumulated coverage at or above which a pixel counts as finished. 0.9961 (= 1 - 1/255) means the light still getting through is under one 8-bit level, which is where the reference 3DGS rasteriser stops. Lowering it skips more work and eventually shows. The accumulation buffer is 16-bit float, whose steps near 1.0 are about 0.0005 wide, so values closer to 1 than that make no difference.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>4</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.005000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="saturationMaskDownscaleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>mask 1/</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>How many screen pixels across one texel of the saturation gate's mask covers. 1 = a full-resolution mask, 4 = a mask with a sixteenth of the pixels. A texel is marked only when every pixel under it is finished, so coarsening the mask never skips a pixel that was still being drawn - the only loss is the partly finished texels around the edge of a saturated region. Making the mask small is what makes checking often affordable. Rebuilds the mask when changed.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>16</number>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <number>4</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QComboBox" name="coverageShrinkModeComboBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Which formula the box to the right feeds. Both read the same coverage estimate and differ only in what they do with it, so switching between them compares the formulas on one frame.

by coverage: the original. Scales the quad's radii by (1 - coverage * value), which narrows the Gaussian along with the quad. Over a half-covered pixel at full strength the splat keeps a quarter of its area, but only half the light through that pixel was still there to lose - so it discards more than the coverage allows, and that overshoot is part of what shows as a band at a slice boundary.

by light loss: the value becomes a budget on light lost instead. Stopping the quad early throws away the splat's own alpha at that radius, attenuated by the light still getting through, so the honest place to stop is where alpha falls to a threshold the coverage sets - the same question the alpha cutoff answers, with a threshold raised by how finished the pixel is. The budget is added to the alpha cutoff rather than replacing it, so a pixel with no coverage is left at exactly the alpha cutoff however high this is set, and the extra light given up is the budget times the coverage. The same Gaussian is drawn over a shorter quad, i.e. the faint edge is truncated rather than the splat being made smaller.

loss capped: the same as "by light loss" with the 1/(1 - coverage) amplification removed, so the threshold is the alpha cutoff plus budget times coverage and can never exceed the alpha cutoff plus the budget. "by light loss" is sound for one splat on its own and wrong for a stack: the budget it licenses is spent again, in full, by every splat over the same pixel, and the amplification makes that ruinous - at coverage 0.99 the factor is 99, so a 0.02 budget asks for a threshold of 2.0 and removes every remaining splat outright instead of trimming its edge. The pixel then freezes at whatever coverage it had reached (0.92 measured on a velvet cushion, against 0.999 without any shrink) and the missing light reads as a hole onto what is behind - one that flickers as the camera turns, because where a pixel freezes depends on where the slice boundaries happened to fall.

Measured at matched draw time against raising the alpha cutoff on its own: a quarter of the error overall, and a seventh of it in the sparsely covered regions where the visible holes are. Both save the same amount on a solid wall; only this one leaves the thin regions alone, because only this one asks whether there was anything there to lose.</string>
        </property>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>by coverage</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>by light loss</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>loss capped</string>
         </property>
        </item>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="coverageShrinkStrengthDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>shrink </string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Diagnostic: instead of the gate's binary keep/drop, shrinks a splat's quad by (1 - coverage * this), where coverage is the mean accumulated coverage already under it (a continuous estimate, read from a mean pyramid built alongside the gate's own). 0 (default, off): no shrink. 1: a splat over an already-fully-covered pixel shrinks to nothing; 0.5 shrinks it by at most half. Requires the gate above to be on - it reads the coverage the gate's own mark pass already computed rather than paying for a pass of its own.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="26" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="layerCapLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Layer cap</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: stop drawing into a pixel once this many splats have blended into it. Counts the layers once for the camera you are standing at, marks the pixels that reached the cap, and then drops splats standing there before any of their work is done - so it costs one counting pass when the camera moves and nothing on the frames after it. The grain is a whole splat, not a pixel: with the conservative test a splat is dropped only where every pixel under it already has its layers, so no hole can open and pixels near the edge of a dense region keep more than the cap. 0 = no cap.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="26" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="layerCapWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="layerCapHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QSpinBox" name="layerCapSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Layers to keep per pixel, nearest first. 0 = no cap. Yields to Clip below when that is ticked - there is only one mask, and an explicit diagnostic wins over this one.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="specialValueText">
         <string>cap off</string>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <number>255</number>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="layerCapOpaqueCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>opaque</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>What a pixel the cap cut short is composited as. Ticked: fully covered, keeping the colour its kept layers averaged to, so the cut shows as a colour error. Unticked: as covered as those layers made it, so the background shows through wherever the cap bit - the quickest way to see where it bit at all.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="checked">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QPushButton" name="layerCapEstimatePushButton">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Estimate</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Measures what a cap at this depth would remove, without capping anything: the frame it runs in draws uncapped, its per-pixel layer counts are read back, and the answer is the share of blended fragments sitting deeper than the cap. That is the ceiling any mechanism cutting at this depth could reach, free of what the mechanism itself would cost. Stalls for a moment; the result appears beside it and in the log.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="layerCapOnCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>on</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Applies the cap beside it, without having to zero the value to switch it off - for A/B against the same camera. Unticked sends no cap at all, so the frame is exactly what it would be without this diagnostic. Not remembered between sessions.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QComboBox" name="hideTestComboBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Which test drops a splat against the mask - applies to the layer cap and to Clip alike. Conservative: only when the splat's whole quad is inside the marked region, so nothing that could still have shown is lost and the picture keeps at least the capped number of layers everywhere. Centre: when the texel under the splat's centre is marked, which cuts through the region and leaves holes - not a picture, but the way to see how much that region was carrying. Quads are bigger than the connected marked patches, so conservative removes far fewer splats.</string>
        </property>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>conservative</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>centre (holes)</string>
         </property>
        </item>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="27" column="1">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="layerCapEstimateResultLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string></string>
     </property>
     <property name="wordWrap">
      <bool>true</bool>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="28" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="coverageCapLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Alpha saturation cap</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: the same per-fragment cut as the layer cap above, but decided by how covered a pixel already is rather than by how many splats have touched it. Front-to-back compositing means a pixel whose accumulated coverage has reached the threshold cannot be changed by anything drawn behind it, so everything still to come there is invisible and can be dropped. Unlike the layer cap this needs no per-pixel counter buffer, which is 8 bytes per fragment of writes the pass no longer pays. Takes the saturation mask over from the gate while it is on - there is only one mask.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="28" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="coverageCapWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="coverageCapHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="coverageCapThresholdDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Accumulated coverage at or above which a pixel stops being composited. Coverage is not a layer count: 1 unit of summed alpha is 63% covered, 2 is 86%, 3 is 95%, 5.5 is 99.6% - so a threshold near 1 is a much later stop than it looks. Separate from the saturation gate's threshold on purpose, so switching between the two does not retune the other. Cuts at slice boundaries, so it needs more than one draw slice.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>4</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.010000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.005000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.960000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="coverageCapOnCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>on</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Applies the cap beside it, without having to clear the value to switch it off - for A/B against the same camera. Yields to the layer cap above when that is on, and stands the saturation gate down while it runs, since all three want the same mask. Not remembered between sessions.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="29" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="ablationStageLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Ablation stage</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: switches the splat pass on one piece at a time, lightest first, so the frame time can be read at each step. The difference between two steps is what the one thing that step added costs. Read the "draw splats" timer, not the frame rate - vsync hides everything. Two caveats: GPU cost is a maximum over the units that saturate rather than a sum of stages, so a step means "the cost of this given everything below it"; and at the CPU-only stage the splat timer reads nothing, so look at CPU frame time there instead.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="29" column="1">
    <widget class="QComboBox" name="ablationStageComboBox">
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Off is the real pass. The stages below it are deliberately wrong pictures - each one is missing everything above it - so nothing here is meant to be left on. Stage 5 is there because going straight from points to full quads moves the vertex maths and the fill at the same time: it runs all of the projection maths and still draws a one-pixel quad, so 5 minus 4 is the vertex work alone and 6 minus 5 is the fill alone.</string>
     </property>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>off (full pass)</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>1 - CPU only, no draw call</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>2 - points, 1 texel, white</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>3 - points, all 4 texels</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>4 - points, splat colour</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>5 - vertex maths, 1px quad</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>6 - full quads, opaque, no blend</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>7 - blending, flat alpha</string>
      </property>
     </item>
     <item>
      <property name="text">
       <string>8 - Gaussian, no discard</string>
      </property>
     </item>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="30" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="quadRadiusScaleLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Quad radius scale</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Diagnostic: multiplies every splat's screen-space radius. 1 is the real size. Rasterised area goes as the square of it, while the splat count, all the vertex work and the length of the blend chain stay exactly the same - so this asks whether a cost is proportional to area at all. A cost that falls by half at 0.7 is fill (rasterising, shading, blend bandwidth); one that does not move is per-splat (instance emission, primitive setup), whatever the stage ladder suggests. Not the same as raising the alpha cutoff, which shrinks each splat by its own opacity instead of scaling them all alike. The Gaussian shrinks with the quad, so splats get smaller rather than hard-edged.&#10;&#10;The two boxes beside it (gamma, ref) make the reduction area-weighted instead of flat: splats at or above the reference radius get the full reduction, smaller ones a tapering fraction of it - see their own tooltips. Both inert while this is 1.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="30" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="quadRadiusScaleWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="quadRadiusScaleHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="pointSizePxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>pt </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> px</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>SESSION071 DIAGNOSTIC: side, in pixels, of the point quad that ablation stages 2-5 emit. Those stages collapse each splat to a single pixel so that fill drops out of the timing, which leaves the point cloud too sparse to read colour off. Widening it gives up the timing meaning of those stages in exchange for a legible picture of the colour each selected splat carries with blending switched off - which is what separates &quot;the bright splats are missing from the selection&quot; from &quot;the bright splats are there and the blend is losing them&quot;. 1 = the original one-pixel behaviour. Only affects stages 2-5; stage 0/6 are untouched.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>64.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="quadRadiusScaleDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>1 = the real size, and the only value the picture is correct at.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.050000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="areaScaleGammaDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Activation speed: how sharply the reduction above concentrates on splats bigger than the reference radius beside it, instead of being applied flatly to every splat regardless of size. 1 = weight is linear in the splat's own screen-space area. Above 1, smaller splats are increasingly spared and the reduction concentrates on the biggest ones; below 1, a partial reduction spreads onto smaller splats too. Inert while Quad radius scale is 1.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>gamma </string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>8.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.100000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="areaScaleRefPxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Reference radius, in screen pixels (before either scale). Splats at or above it get the full Quad radius scale reduction; smaller ones get a fraction of it, tapering off at the rate gamma sets. Inert while Quad radius scale is 1.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="prefix">
         <string>ref </string>
        </property>
        <property name="suffix">
         <string> px</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>0</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>1.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>5.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>20.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="31" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="bufferTricksSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Buffer tricks</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>SESSION073: grouped the accumulation-buffer knobs (previously split across two rows) with the area-slice diagnostic that shares the same buffer - all of it is about what goes into, and how it comes back out of, the splat pass's own accumulation buffer.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="32" column="0" colspan="2">
    <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="bufferTricksHorizontalLayout">
     <item>
      <widget class="QCheckBox" name="accumBuffer8BitCheckBox">
       <property name="text">
        <string>8 bit</string>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Blends the splats into an RGBA8 buffer instead of the default RGBA16F, halving the bytes each fragment writes. The splat pass is mostly bound by blend bandwidth, so this is worth about a third of it. The cost is precision: hundreds of blends accumulate per pixel and the resolve then divides the accumulated colour by the accumulated coverage, so quantisation is amplified where coverage is low - cloud silhouettes and thin sparse regions, not the dense areas. It also weakens the saturation gate, whose saving is the blending it avoids. Rebuilds the accumulation framebuffer when toggled.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="accumBufferScaleDoubleSpinBox">
       <property name="prefix">
        <string>scale </string>
       </property>
       <property name="decimals">
        <number>2</number>
       </property>
       <property name="minimum">
        <double>0.100000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="maximum">
        <double>1.000000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="singleStep">
        <double>0.050000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="value">
        <double>1.000000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Renders the splats into an accumulation buffer this fraction of the frame's size, then scales the result back up in the resolve. The splat pass is bound by blend bandwidth, which scales with pixels: on the interior scene, blending alone is about 9 ms of a 17 ms frame, so 0.5 (a quarter of the pixels) takes it to roughly 9 ms total. The LoD selection deliberately does not follow this - the same splats are drawn, just into a smaller buffer - so the ~6.3 ms of per-splat vertex and instance work is untouched and is the floor of this knob whatever the scale. Returns bend before that floor: at pixel scale limit 2 the LoD brings splats to about 2 px, so at half scale they land on the vertex shader's half-pixel low-pass floor and area falls by about 3.3x rather than 4x. 1 = off, and every path then behaves exactly as it did without this setting. Rebuilds the accumulation framebuffer when changed.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QComboBox" name="accumUpsampleModeComboBox">
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>SESSION073: how the resolve reads a downscaled accumulation buffer back up to the frame - see each item's own tooltip. Ignored at scale 1, where the buffer is indexed one-to-one.</string>
       </property>
       <item>
        <property name="text">
         <string>nearest</string>
        </property>
       </item>
       <item>
        <property name="text">
         <string>bilinear</string>
        </property>
       </item>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QComboBox" name="areaSliceModeComboBox">
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>DIAGNOSTIC. Draws only one half of the cloud, split by each splat's own projected area on screen. This is the axis the pass is actually paid in - blended pixels - unlike the size and distance slices further down. On the interior scene the splats above 256 px of area are 13% of the cloud but 85% of the fill, and they are also the smooth ones that survive a heavy downscale, so the two halves are very nearly "almost all of the cost" and "almost all of the detail". Draw each alone, at its own buffer scale, to see what a two-layer split would cost and look like before it is built. Threshold is in frame pixels, matching the Frustum report's "Fill by splat size" histogram, and is converted internally so it selects the same splats at any buffer scale.</string>
       </property>
       <item>
        <property name="text">
         <string>all splats</string>
        </property>
       </item>
       <item>
        <property name="text">
         <string>only large</string>
        </property>
       </item>
       <item>
        <property name="text">
         <string>only small</string>
        </property>
       </item>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="areaSlicePxDoubleSpinBox">
       <property name="prefix">
        <string>at </string>
       </property>
       <property name="suffix">
        <string> px</string>
       </property>
       <property name="decimals">
        <number>0</number>
       </property>
       <property name="minimum">
        <double>1.000000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="maximum">
        <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="singleStep">
        <double>64.000000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="value">
        <double>256.000000000000000</double>
       </property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Area of one splat, in frame pixels, that divides "large" from "small" for the slice beside this. Read it off the Frustum report's "Fill by splat size" histogram, whose buckets are in the same unit. 256 px (a radius of about 9 px) is where the measured split puts 13% of the splats and 85% of the fill on the large side.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item row="33" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QFrame" name="diagnosticsSeparatorLine">
     <property name="frameShape">
      <enum>QFrame::HLine</enum>
     </property>
     <property name="frameShadow">
      <enum>QFrame::Sunken</enum>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="34" column="0" colspan="2">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="diagnosticsSectionLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Diagnostics</string>
     </property>
     <property name="styleSheet">
      <string notr="true">font-weight: bold;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Measures or exposes what is there. Nothing here is meant to be left on.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="35" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="sizeClampLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Size clamp min/max (m)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Debug tool for locating abnormally large/degenerate splats (e.g. under-reconstructed scene edges). Hides any splat whose feature size (2 x its largest scale axis, in metres) falls outside [min, max]. 0 disables that bound (same "0 = unlimited" convention as the Splats list search radius) - default (0, 0) has no effect. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="35" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="sizeClampWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="sizeClampHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="sizeClampMinDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Splats with feature size below this (metres) are hidden. Applies live. 0 = no lower cull.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="sizeClampMaxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Splats with feature size above this (metres) are hidden. Applies live. 0 (default) = no upper cull.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>3</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>1000000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.10000000000000001</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="sizeClampInvertCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Invert</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Unchecked (default): hide splats outside [min, max] - isolate a size range. Checked: hide splats inside [min, max] instead - exclude a size range, leaving the rest of the cloud untouched. No effect while both bounds are 0.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="36" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="distClampLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Distance slice min/max (m)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Slices the cloud open by distance from the camera, so you can look inside it a shell at a time. Shows only splats whose distance from the camera lies inside [min, max] metres; tick Invert to hide that shell instead and see through it. The distance is measured from the camera position, so the slice is a spherical shell and turning on the spot does not change what survives. Requires the checkbox to its left to be ticked - unchecked, these fields have no effect. SESSION072: when enabled, applies both at LoD-traversal time (whole out-of-range subtrees pruned before the CPU pipeline spends anything on them) and in the vertex shader (the final per-splat cut). Applies live either way, no restart needed.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="36" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="distClampWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="distClampHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="distClampEnabledCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string></string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Enable/disable the distance-slice clamping functionality (min/max/Invert below). Unchecked (default): fully inert - no effect on what is drawn, in the shader or the traversal. SESSION072: checked also prunes whole out-of-range subtrees at LoD-traversal time (not just discarding per-splat in the shader afterwards), so isolating a shell no longer pays the full CPU/GPU cost of everything outside it.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="distClampMinDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.250000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QDoubleSpinBox" name="distClampMaxDoubleSpinBox">
        <property name="decimals">
         <number>2</number>
        </property>
        <property name="minimum">
         <double>0.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="maximum">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="singleStep">
         <double>0.250000000000000</double>
        </property>
        <property name="value">
         <double>10000.000000000000000</double>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="distClampInvertCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Invert</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Unchecked (default): show only what is inside [min, max]. Checked: hide that shell and show everything outside it - which is how you cut away the near wall and look into the room behind it.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="37" column="0">
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="showDebugCheckBox">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Show debug</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Replaces splat colour with a flat additive increment per surviving fragment, so the picture burns out where many splats pile onto the same pixel. What is accumulated depends on the mode beside this box, and the two answer different questions:&#10;&#10;Overdraw - sums 1.0 per fragment, giving depth complexity: how many splats a pixel has to blend. That is what drives blend cost, rather than raw splat count.&#10;&#10;Alpha - sums each fragment's alpha instead, so the ramp shows accumulated alpha per pixel. Read against the layer count from the same spot it sizes up a front-to-back transmittance cutoff, the early-out a hardware blend pipeline cannot do per fragment: the share of fragment work such a cutoff could skip is roughly (summed alpha) / 5.5. Hundreds of layers beside a small alpha sum means near-threshold splats a cutoff would never reach - that case needs pruning, not early-out.&#10;&#10;Alpha sums are far smaller numbers than layer counts, so set the range below to match the mode. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="37" column="1">
    <widget class="QWidget" name="debugModeWidget" native="true">
     <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="debugModeHorizontalLayout">
      <property name="leftMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="topMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="rightMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <property name="bottomMargin">
       <number>0</number>
      </property>
      <item>
       <widget class="QComboBox" name="debugModeComboBox">
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Which measure the debug ramp and Clip work on. See the tooltip on "Show debug" for what each one answers.&#10;&#10;The "Coverage Ln" entries are a different kind of view: instead of a ramp over what the splats produced, they draw the coverage pyramid the shrink reads, in grey - black is nothing covered, white is finished. Level 0 is one texel per "mask 1/n" pixels; each level up is twice as coarse, and the coarse levels are the ones a big splat's quad actually samples. Flipping the reduce mode beside Draw slices between mean and min while watching one of these is what shows the difference the switch makes. Needs the saturation gate on, since its mark pass is what builds the pyramid; Clip and the range boxes do nothing in these modes.</string>
        </property>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Overdraw</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Alpha</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Coverage L1</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Coverage L3</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Saturation mask</string>
         </property>
        </item>
        <item>
         <property name="text">
          <string>Saturation ramp</string>
         </property>
        </item>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="clipCheckBox">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Clip</string>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Throws away every splat standing where the chosen measure has reached the range maximum - exactly the region the ramp paints solid red - and draws what is left, so "are the red regions really where the time goes" can be answered by removing them and looking at the clock instead of by inference. Follows the mode beside it: layers with Overdraw selected, accumulated alpha with Alpha.&#10;&#10;The splats are dropped in the vertex shader, before rasterisation. It costs an extra counting pass over the splats, so the frame total is meaningless while this is on - read the "draw splats" GPU timer, which starts after that pass and so measures the same thing in both states. Switches the saturation gate off while it is on; they share the same mask.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item row="38" column="0">
    <widget class="QLabel" name="overdrawRangeLabel">
     <property name="text">
      <string>Overdraw range (layers)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Layer-count range the overdraw ramp above maps to blue (min) .. green (midpoint) .. red (max). E.g. [2, 100]: pixels with 2 or fewer overlapping splats are solid blue, 100+ are solid red, in between fades smoothly. Applies live.</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
